WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:03.255
Track C starts here — local models running on your own hardware, under the app's supervision.

2
00:00:03.255 --> 00:00:07.000
This episode: load a downloaded model, tune the basics, and use it from chat exactly like a cloud provider.

3
00:00:07.000 --> 00:00:09.229
Once a model is downloaded it shows up in My Models.

4
00:00:09.229 --> 00:00:10.643
From there it's one button: Load.

5
00:00:10.643 --> 00:00:16.000
The app spawns a supervised llama-server for it in the background, and Eject shuts that server down cleanly when you're done.

6
00:00:16.000 --> 00:00:17.407
Two controls matter before you load.

7
00:00:17.407 --> 00:00:21.160
Context length sets how much conversation the model can hold at once — bigger costs more memory.

8
00:00:21.160 --> 00:00:28.000
GPU offload layers decides how many of the model's layers run on your GPU versus the CPU; more offloaded layers usually means faster generation, up to what your VRAM can hold.

9
00:00:28.000 --> 00:00:38.000
As you adjust those two controls, an estimated memory readout updates live — so you can see you're about to overshoot your VRAM before you hit Load, not after it fails.

10
00:00:38.000 --> 00:00:38.821
Here's the payoff.

11
00:00:38.821 --> 00:00:43.384
Open the chat's provider picker and there's now a Local entry, right alongside your cloud providers.

12
00:00:43.384 --> 00:00:50.000
Pick it, and you're talking to the model you just loaded — through the exact same OpenAI-compatible client the app uses for every cloud provider.

13
00:00:50.000 --> 00:00:53.546
That's the whole point of the design: local isn't a special code path bolted on the side.

14
00:00:53.546 --> 00:01:00.000
It's just another endpoint behind the same interface every other provider uses, which is why switching between local and cloud mid-project is completely seamless.

15
00:01:00.000 --> 00:01:01.138
Loading and running is the easy part.

16
00:01:01.138 --> 00:01:06.000
Next we go under the hood, into the actual llama-server flags this app can pass — the advanced tuning for squeezing real performance out of specific hardware.

